r a Series.\n1557 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1558 | \n1559 |         Examples\n1560 |         --------\n1561 |         In the example below we have a DataFrame with the information about\n1562 |         planet's mass and radius. We pass the 'mass' column to the\n1563 |         pie function to get a pie plot.\n1564 | \n1565 |         .. plot::\n1566 |             :context: close-figs\n1567 | \n1568 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'mass': [0.330, 4.87 , 5.97],\n1569 |             ...                    'radius': [2439.7, 6051.8, 6378.1]},\n1570 |             ...                   index=['Mercury', 'Venus', 'Earth'])\n1571 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(y='mass', figsize=(5, 5))\n1572 | \n1573 |         .. plot::\n1574 |             :context: close-figs\n1575 | \n1576 |             >>> plot = df.plot.pie(subplots=True, figsize=(11, 6))\n1577 |         \"\"\"\n1578 |         if (\n1579 |             isinstance(self._parent, ABCDataFrame)\n1580 |             and kwargs.get(\"y\", None) is None\n1581 |             and not kwargs.get(\"subplots\", False)\n1582 |         ):\n1583 |             raise ValueError(\"pie requires either y column or 'subplots=True'\")\n1584 |         return self(kind=\"pie\", **kwargs)\n1585 | \n1586 |     def scatter(self, x, y, s=None, c=None, **kwargs):\n1587 |         \"\"\"\n1588 |         Create a scatter plot with varying marker point size and color.\n1589 | \n1590 |         The coordinates of each point are defined by two dataframe columns and\n1591 |         filled circles are used to represent each point. This kind of plot is\n1592 |         useful to see complex correlations between two variables. Points could\n1593 |         be for instance natural 2D coordinates like longitude and latitude in\n1594 |         a map or, in general, any pair of metrics that can be plotted against\n1595 |         each other.\n1596 | \n1597 |         Parameters\n1598 |         ----------\n1599 |         x : int or str\n1600 |             The column name or column position to be used as horizontal\n1601 |             coordinates for each point.\n1602 |         y : int or str\n1603 |             The column name or column position to be used as vertical\n1604 |             coordinates for each point.\n1605 |         s : str, scalar or array-like, optional\n1606 |             The size of each point. Possible values are:\n1607 | \n1608 |             - A string with the name of the column to be used for marker's size.\n1609 | \n1610 |             - A single scalar so all points have the same size.\n1611 | \n1612 |             - A sequence of scalars, which will be used for each point's size\n1613 |               recursively. For instance, when passing [2,14] all points size\n1614 |               will be either 2 or 14, alternatively.\n1615 | \n1616 |               .. versionchanged:: 1.1.0\n1617 | \n1618 |         size : str, scalar or array-like, optional\n1619 |             Alias for s.\n1620 | \n1621 |             .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n1622 | \n1623 |         c : str, int or array-like, optional\n1624 |             The color of each point. Possible values are:\n1625 | \n1626 |             - A single color string referred to by name, RGB or RGBA code,\n1627 |               for instance 'red' or '#a98d19'.\n1628 | \n1629 |             - A sequence of color strings referred to by name, RGB or RGBA\n1630 |               code, which will be used for each point's color recursively. For\n1631 |               instance ['green','yellow'] all points will be filled in green or\n1632 |               yellow, alternatively.\n1633 | \n1634 |             - A column name or position whose values will be used to color the\n1635 |               marker points according to a colormap.\n1636 |         color : str, int or array-like, optional\n1637 |             Alias for c.\n1638 | \n1639 |             .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n1640 | \n1641 |         **kwargs\n1642 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1643 | \n1644 |         Returns\n1645 |         -------\n1646 |         :class:`matplotlib.axes.Axes` or numpy.ndarray of them\n1647 | \n1648 |         See Also\n1649 |         --------\n1650 |         matplotlib.pyplot.scatter : Scatter plot using multiple input data\n1651 |             formats.\n1652 | \n1653 |         Examples\n1654 |         --------\n1655 |         Let's see how to draw a scatter plot using coordinates from the values\n1656 |         in a DataFrame's columns.\n1657 | \n1658 |         .. plot::\n1659 |             :context: close-figs\n1660 | \n1661 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[5.1, 3.5, 0], [4.9, 3.0, 0], [7.0, 3.2, 1],\n1662 |             ...                    [6.4, 3.2, 1], [5.9, 3.0, 2]],\n1663 |             ...                   columns=['length', 'width', 'species'])\n1664 |             >>> ax1 = df.plot.scatter(x='length',\n1665 |             ...                       y='width',\n1666 |             ...                       c='DarkBlue')\n1667 | \n1668 |         And now with the color determined by a column as well.\n1669 | \n1670 |         .. plot::\n1671 |             :context: close-figs\n1672 | \n1673 |             >>> ax2 = df.plot.scatter(x='length',\n1674 |             ...                       y='width',\n1675 |             ...                       c='species',\n1676 |             ...                       colormap='viridis')\n1677 |         \"\"\"\n1678 |         size = kwargs.pop(\"size\", None)\n1679 |         if s is not None and size is not None:\n1680 |             raise TypeError(\"Specify exactly one of `s` and `size`\")\n1681 |         elif s is not None or size is not None:\n1682 |             kwargs[\"s\"] = s if s is not None else size\n1683 | \n1684 |         color = kwargs.pop(\"color\", None)\n1685 |         if c is not None and color is not None:\n1686 |             raise TypeError(\"Specify exactly one of `c` and `color`\")\n1687 |         elif c is not None or color is not None:\n1688 |             kwargs[\"c\"] = c if c is not None else color\n1689 | \n1690 |         return self(kind=\"scatter\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n1691 | \n1692 |     def hexbin(self, x, y, C=None, reduce_C_function=None, gridsize=None, **kwargs):\n1693 |         \"\"\"\n1694 |         Generate a hexagonal binning plot.\n1695 | \n1696 |         Generate a hexagonal binning plot of `x` versus `y`. If `C` is `None`\n1697 |         (the default), this is a histogram of the number of occurrences\n1698 |         of the observations at ``(x[i], y[i])``.\n1699 | \n1700 |         If `C` is specified, specifies values at given coordinates\n1701 |         ``(x[i], y[i])``. These values are accumulated for each hexagonal\n1702 |         bin and then reduced according to `reduce_C_function`,\n1703 |         having as default the NumPy's mean function (:meth:`numpy.mean`).\n1704 |         (If `C` is specified, it must also be a 1-D sequence\n1705 |         of the same length as `x` and `y`, or a column label.)\n1706 | \n1707 |         Parameters\n1708 |         ----------\n1709 |         x : int or str\n1710 |             The column label or position for x points.\n1711 |         y : int or str\n1712 |             The column label or position for y points.\n1713 |         C : int or str, optional\n1714 |             The column label or position for the value of `(x, y)` point.\n1715 |         reduce_C_function : callable, default `np.mean`\n1716 |             Function of one argument that reduces all the values in a bin to\n1717 |             a single number (e.g. `np.mean`, `np.max`, `np.sum`, `np.std`).\n1718 |         gridsize : int or tuple of (int, int), default 100\n1719 |             The number of hexagons in the x-direction.\n1720 |             The corresponding number of hexagons in the y-direction is\n1721 |             chosen in a way that the hexagons are approximately regular.\n1722 |             Alternatively, gridsize can be a tuple with two elements\n1723 |             specifying the number of hexagons in the x-direction and the\n1724 |             y-direction.\n1725 |         **kwargs\n1726 |             Additional keyword arguments are documented in\n1727 |             :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1728 | \n1729 |         Returns\n1730 |         -------\n1731 |         matplotlib.AxesSubplot\n1732 |             The matplotlib ``Axes`` on which the hexbin is plotted.\n1733 | \n1734 |         See Also\n1735 |         --------\n1736 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1737 |         matplotlib.pyplot.hexbin : Hexagonal binning plot using matplotlib,\n1738 |             the matplotlib function that is used under the hood.\n1739 | \n1740 |         Examples\n1741 |         --------\n1742 |         The following examples are generated with random data from\n1743 |         a normal distribution.\n1744 | \n1745 |         .. plot::\n1746 |             :context: close-figs\n1747 | \n1748 |             >>> n = 10000\n1749 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'x': np.random.randn(n),\n1750 |             ...                    'y': np.random.randn(n)})\n1751 |             >>> ax = df.plot.hexbin(x='x', y='y', gridsize=20)\n1752 | \n1753 |         The next example uses `C` and `np.sum` as `reduce_C_function`.\n1754 |         Note that `'observations'` values ranges from 1 to 5 but the result\n1755 |         plot shows values up to more than 25. This is because of the\n1756 |         `reduce_C_function`.\n1757 | \n1758 |         .. plot::\n1759 |             :context: close-figs\n1760 | \n1761 |             >>> n = 500\n1762 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1763 |             ...     'coord_x': np.random.uniform(-3, 3, size=n),\n1764 |             ...     'coord_y': np.random.uniform(30, 50, size=n),\n1765 |             ...     'observations': np.random.randint(1,5, size=n)\n1766 |             ...     })\n1767 |             >>> ax = df.plot.hexbin(x='coord_x',\n1768 |             ...                     y='co