4, 4)]\n3738 |     X, y = make_blobs(\n3739 |         n_samples=100,\n3740 |         random_state=0,\n3741 |         n_features=4,\n3742 |         centers=centers,\n3743 |         cluster_std=1.0,\n3744 |         shuffle=True,\n3745 |     )\n3746 |     X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n3747 |         X, y, test_size=0.2, random_state=0\n3748 |     )\n3749 |     estimator = clone(estimator_orig)\n3750 | \n3751 |     if hasattr(estimator, \"decision_function\") and hasattr(estimator, \"predict_proba\"):\n3752 |         estimator.fit(X_train, y_train)\n3753 |         # Since the link function from decision_function() to predict_proba()\n3754 |         # is sometimes not precise enough (typically expit), we round to the\n3755 |         # 10th decimal to avoid numerical issues: we compare the rank\n3756 |         # with deterministic ties rather than get platform specific rank\n3757 |         # inversions in case of machine level differences.\n3758 |         a = estimator.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1].round(decimals=10)\n3759 |         b = estimator.decision_function(X_test).round(decimals=10)\n3760 | \n3761 |         rank_proba, rank_score = rankdata(a), rankdata(b)\n3762 |         try:\n3763 |             assert_array_almost_equal(rank_proba, rank_score)\n3764 |         except AssertionError:\n3765 |             # Sometimes, the rounding applied on the probabilities will have\n3766 |             # ties that are not present in the scores because it is\n3767 |             # numerically more precise. In this case, we relax the test by\n3768 |             # grouping the decision function scores based on the probability\n3769 |             # rank and check that the score is monotonically increasing.\n3770 |             grouped_y_score = np.array(\n3771 |                 [b[rank_proba == group].mean() for group in np.unique(rank_proba)]\n3772 |             )\n3773 |             sorted_idx = np.argsort(grouped_y_score)\n3774 |             assert_array_equal(sorted_idx, np.arange(len(sorted_idx)))\n```\n\nFound function `doc/api_reference.py:_get_submodule`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n21 | def _get_submodule(module_name, submodule_name):\n22 |     \"\"\"Get the submodule docstring and automatically add the hook.\n23 | \n24 |     `module_name` is e.g. `sklearn.feature_extraction`, and `submodule_name` is e.g.\n25 |     `image`, so we get the docstring and hook for `sklearn.feature_extraction.image`\n26 |     submodule. `module_name` is used to reset the current module because autosummary\n27 |     automatically changes the current module.\n28 |     \"\"\"\n29 |     lines = [\n30 |         f\".. automodule:: {module_name}.{submodule_name}\",\n31 |         f\".. currentmodule:: {module_name}\",\n32 |     ]\n33 |     return \"\\n\\n\".join(lines)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/feature_extraction/_hash.py:FeatureHasher.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n128 | def fit(self, X=None, y=None):\n129 |         \"\"\"Only validates estimator's parameters.\n130 | \n131 |         This method allows to: (i) validate the estimator's parameters and\n132 |         (ii) be consistent with the scikit-learn transformer API.\n133 | \n134 |         Parameters\n135 |         ----------\n136 |         X : Ignored\n137 |             Not used, present here for API consistency by convention.\n138 | \n139 |         y : Ignored\n140 |             Not used, present here for API consistency by convention.\n141 | \n142 |         Returns\n143 |         -------\n144 |         self : object\n145 |             FeatureHasher class instance.\n146 |         \"\"\"\n147 |         return self\n```\n"}, {"content": "Given that `assert_consistent_docs` is not found in any of the search results, we need to make an educated guess about where it might be located. Since the issue is about adding a function to check for consistency between docstrings of objects, it is likely that this function is located in a file dedicated to testing or utilities for docstrings, possibly in `sklearn/utils/` or `sklearn/utils/tests/`.\n\nLet's retrieve the contents of `sklearn/utils/docstring.py` and `sklearn/utils/tests/test_docstring.py` to see if they contain any documentation-related utilities or tests. We will then analyze these files to find the location of `assert_consistent_docs`.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/utils/docstring.py', 'sklearn/utils/tests/test_docstring.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/utils/docstring.py`...\n### Search Result:\nInvalid name. \nHint: Valid entity name should be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" or just \"file_path\".\n\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/utils/tests/test_docstring.py`...\n### Search Result:\nInvalid name. \nHint: Valid entity name should be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" or just \"file_path\".\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "The Maximum number of interation has been reached, please generate your final output with required format and use <finish></finish> to exit."}, {"content": "```\nsklearn/utils/tests/test_docstring.py\nline: 1\nline: 2\nline: 3\nline: 4\nline: 5\n```\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nYour last answer did not follow the required final output format.\n\nRewrite your final answer now using this exact structure:\n\n```\npath/to/file1.py\nline: 10-20\nclass: MyClass\nfunction: my_function\n\npath/to/file2.py\nline: 42\nfunction: another_function\n```\n<finish></finish>\n\nRules:\n- Output ONLY one fenced code block, then `<finish></finish>`\n- Each file path must be on its own line and end with `.py`\n- Do NOT use `file.py:QualifiedName` on one line\n- Do NOT include explanations,